Unit Testing is the steel framework that ensures it doesn't collapse when the wind blows.

Testing Philosophy: The "AAA" Pattern
Every professional unit test follows the Arrange, Act, Assert pattern:
1. Arrange: Set up the test data and the objects.
2. Act: Call the exact method you are trying to test.
3. Assert: Prove that the result matches what you expected.

Step 1: The Project Setup
Add dependencies:

<dependencies>
    <!-- 1. JUnit 5 (The Test Engine) -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
        <artifactId>junit-jupiter-engine</artifactId>
        <version>6.1.3</version>
        <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- 2. Mockito (The Fake Object Generator) -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.mockito</groupId>
        <artifactId>mockito-core</artifactId>
        <version>5.23.0</version>
        <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- 3. Mockito JUnit 5 Integration -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.mockito</groupId>
        <artifactId>mockito-junit-jupiter</artifactId>
        <version>5.23.0</version>
        <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>

Step 2: The Absolute Basics (Pure JUnit)
In Maven, your actual code goes in src/main/java, and your tests go in src/test/java.
so, write code in main.

Step 3: Writing Your First Unit Test
Now, go to src/test/java and create the test class.
Notice how we use the @Test annotation so JUnit knows this method is a test, and
assertEquals to prove the math is correct.

Run the Test!

If you are in IntelliJ, there will be a little green Play button next to CalculatorTest
class. Click it.
You will see the glorious Green Checkmarks indicating your tests passed. If you change
assertEquals(12, result) to assertEquals(99, result), the test will fail and turn Red.
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
What we just did was "Level 1". If you only know @Test and assertEquals, your test files
will quickly become massive, repetitive, and fragile.
To master pure JUnit 5, we need to conquer three advanced concepts before we even touch
Mockito:
1. The Test Lifecycle (Preventing Test Contamination)
2. Parameterized Tests (Data-Driven Testing)
3. Nested Tests (BDD Style Organization)
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
Advanced Concept 1: The Test Lifecycle & Test Contamination
The Problem: Imagine you are testing a ShoppingCart.
Test A adds an Apple. Test B adds a Banana and checks if the cart size is 1.
If JUnit runs Test A first, and they share the same cart in memory, Test B will fail
because the cart size is actually 2! This is called Test Contamination. Every test must
run in a 100% isolated vacuum.

The Solution: Lifecycle Annotations.
1. @BeforeEach: Runs before every single test. Use this to give every test a fresh,
   empty object.
2. @AfterEach: Runs after every single test. Use this to clean up databases or close
   files.
3. @BeforeAll / @AfterAll: Runs exactly once for the whole file. (Must be static).
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
Advanced Concept 2: Parameterized Tests (The Code-Killer)
The Problem: You have a method that calculates discounts based on user age. You want to
test 5 different ages.
The Junior Developer writes 5 separate @Test methods, copy-pasting the exact same code
5 times, just changing the numbers.

The Senior Solution: @ParameterizedTest.
You write the test logic exactly once, and feed it a CSV table of data. JUnit will
automatically loop through the data and run the test 5 times!
(Note: To use this, you must add one more dependency to your pom.xml):

    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
        <artifactId>junit-jupiter-params</artifactId>
        <version>5.11.0</version>
        <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
Advanced Concept 3: @Nested (Behavior-Driven Development)
The Problem: Imagine testing a BankAccount class. You need to test what happens when
you withdraw money.
But the behavior of withdraw() completely changes based on the context:
1. What if the account is brand new?
2. What if the account has sufficient funds?
3. What if the account is overdrawn?

If you write 15 flat tests like testWithdrawWhenEmpty(), testWithdrawWhenFull(), the
file becomes an unreadable mess.

The Senior Solution: @Nested.
You create inner classes to group your tests logically by Context. The true magic is
that the inner class can have its own @BeforeEach setup, while still inheriting the
@BeforeEach from the parent class!
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
Advanced Concept 4: Senior Assertions
assertEquals is great, but real enterprise systems return Lists, complex Objects, and
have strict performance requirements.